{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"d8c426f1-8ffa-4108-ae9d-54c70f3096eb","status":"SUCCESS","response":"I have fixed the issue where users see the incorrect dates on their posts. \n\n### What was going wrong\n1. **Time conversion was flawed**: `display_date()` was taking the post's server-generated UTC date, appending \"12:00 UTC\" to it, and then attempting to convert that to the viewer's timezone. This logic forced the viewer to almost always see the exact same date as the server's UTC date, instead of the real local date relative to when the post was created. \n2. **Feed sorting was imprecise**: `feed()` sorted by the UTC date (`post[\"date\"]`) as the primary key. If multiple posts were made on the same day, it fell back to sorting by author name alphabetically instead of chronologically!\n\n### How I fixed it\n- **`display_date()`**: Now uses the exact `created_at` timestamp of the post, which allows it to accurately convert the real time of creation directly to the viewer's timezone and show the correct local date.\n- **`feed()`**: Now sorts by the `created_at` timestamp instead of the `date` string, guaranteeing that posts on the same day are correctly sorted chronologically (newest first). \n\nI've merged these changes and updated your test suite with two new tests covering both cases, all of which now run successfully (`python3 -m unittest discover -s tests`).\n","duration_seconds":143.364616,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":64981,"output_tokens":15376,"thinking_tokens":10987,"cache_read_tokens":218987,"total_tokens":80357}}}
